   "authorText": "@fuzzyfriendsrescuevoluntee3036",
   "authorChannelId": "UCQRJVx6uP8Lc-jVmV7uuzPQ",
   "authorThumbnail": [],
   "textDisplay": "Hubby is a dr - he says if a brand says \"clinically tested\" this means the product is confirmed not to work because if it worked, it would say \"clinically proven\" BUT he also says that still really means very little, because if something actually worked well it'd boast \"peer reviewed studies\" on their website. So.. \"clinically tested\" means zilch. \"clinically proven\" means they got a small group of people, gave them a free product to try & their results were recorded in a clinical setting. But again, the brand is the one paying for the study so the clinicians and participants will likely say whatever the brand wants them to say. \"Peer reviewed study\" means the data is actually valid. Hope this helps! :)",
